  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nForecast area remains on the northwestern periphery of an \nupper-level low which is progged to deepen across the mid- \nAtlantic region over the next couple of days. GOES water vapor \nimagery currently indicates a smaller-amplitude short wave \nwrapping west-southwest across lower MI/LM and IN around the \nback side of this system. The main impact of this disturbance on\nour forecast area is to continue to spread an expansive area of\nlow clouds to the west across the cwa early this morning \n(though some large breaks have developed toward the IL/WI \nborder), with some patchy drizzle reported earlier south of the \nI-80 corridor into central IL which should end with the passage \nof the disturbance this morning. Mostly cloudy skies are \nexpected today especially for our south/southeastern cwa. While\nsome significant breaks are likely farther north, increasing \nhigh clouds are expected later this afternoon as another short \nwave tracks into the upper Midwest from the Northern Plains. \nOtherwise, less-breezy conditions are expected today with high \ntemps in the upper 60s to lower 70s (mid-60s along the Lake \nMichigan shore).\n\nVarious model guidance indicates the potential for some spotty \nshowers to spread into far north/northwest IL toward daybreak \nFriday, in association with the aforementioned short wave \npropagating into the upper Midwest. This wave will be running \ninto short wave ridging aloft on the northwestern fringe of the \ndeepening east coast low however, which along with easterly \nlow-level dry air advection is expected to limit the eastward \ndevelopment of precipitation. Have included some slight chance \n(<=20%) pops for these decaying showers mainly west of the Fox \nRiver Valley across northern and northwest IL between about \n4AM-10AM Friday morning. \n\nBehind Friday's wave, the narrow upper-level ridge is then \nprogged to briefly redevelop into the upper-Midwest Friday night\ninto Saturday before another short wave approaches the upper- \nMidwest Saturday evening. Similar to Friday morning's scenario, \nthis wave will also be fighting the ridge and dry easterly low- \nlevel flow. Some decaying showers will again be possible \nSaturday night across our far western counties of the forecast \narea, where again some slight chance (<20%) pops are in place \nwest of I-39. Daytime hours look to be dry over the weekend, \nthough due to the blocking of the pattern of the deep east coast\nupper low, our anticipated gradual warming trend looks to be on\nhold. Partly to mostly cloudy skies and continued modest east- \nnortheast surface winds will likely keep our temperatures \nslightly below average in the mid-60s to lower 70s both \nSaturday and Sunday.\n\nA slower evolution is not unusual in such highly-amplified \nupper air patterns, and the latest global model/ensemble \nsolutions have delayed kicking out the eastern low until early-\nmid next week. As this occurs, there are indications that the \npattern will become more active with an eastward-moving frontal\nzone and surface low developing into the region toward mid- \nweek. NBM initialization depicts this with 40-60% pops later \nTuesday into Wednesday and made no changes at this distance, \nthough there is quite a bit of ensemble spread in the current\n00Z EPS/GEFS/CMCE solutions with the timing/strength and \nplacement of features at this time.\n\nRatzer",
